  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "sim-05-decimal-simplification-and-approximation",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Decimal Simplification and Approximate Value",
    "description": "Line up, multiply, and divide decimals accurately inside BODMAS expressions, and estimate answers when the exam asks for an approximate value.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build decimal skill layer by layer — aligned addition and subtraction, place-counting multiplication, shift-both-sides division — then show the decimal-to-fraction conversion trick, decimals inside BODMAS, the exam's approximate-value instruction, and a missing-number decimal capstone. Each idea gets a worked example, a fresh question, and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 27,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach decimal addition and subtraction by aligning the decimal point, multiplication by counting total decimal places, and division by a decimal after shifting both numbers by the same power of ten to make the divisor whole. Show how converting a decimal to a fraction (5.25 = 21/4) can make a BODMAS expression with fractions much easier, and handle decimals inside mixed expressions such as 4.5 + 3.2 × 2 − 1.4. Cover the exam's 'approximate value' instruction where exact calculation is explicitly not expected: round each number to a nearby friendly value, compute, and choose the closest option, as in 19.99 × 15.98 + 224.98 + 125.02 ≈ 670. End with missing-number decimal equations such as 128.43 + 30.21 + ? = 173. Learners should place the decimal point by estimation as a check, not by counting alone."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make decimals feel as easy as whole numbers.\n\nFour small habits — align the point, count the places, shift both sides, and round when told — will carry you through every decimal question in this chapter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make decimals behave.\n\nA decimal point is a fence between whole numbers and parts. To add or subtract, line up the fences:\n\n23.45\n 6.7\n 0.089\n\nStacked by the point, the columns take care of themselves.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Fill empty places with zeros and add:\n\n23.450\n 6.700\n 0.089\n────────\n30.239\n\nEach column is just normal addition. The point in the answer sits directly below the other points.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
